motive with a human heart in it. a coded recipe book was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone.\n\ngardener Old Wick kept watch from the garden gate while Clara Button asked about a coded recipe book. the masked visitor claimed never to have seen it — but described it perfectly, down to the scratch on its side. Clara Button noted that, too.'}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "They walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. gardener Old Wick said nothing, but their silence was agreement. courage in quiet lives — even on the worst night, Clara Button thought, especially then.\n\nThe storm that had threatened Hollyhock Lane all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Hollyhock Lane. In the midst of it came a hammering at Clara Button's door: gardener Old Wick, soaked, with news — the masked visitor had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nClara Button listened to the end, rain running off her cape. Justice, she said quietly, was not the same as punishment; but the truth belonged to everyone in Hollyhock Lane, and it would be told."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "The constable did his duty, but Clara Button had already done hers: she had made the truth plain, so that no whisper could twist it. gardener Old Wick squeezed her hand. 'Neatest case I ever saw,' they whispered.\n\na coded recipe book was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Clara Button declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nThat evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Clara Button wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. courage in quiet lives — I shall drink tea to that."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'the masked visitor, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Clara Button read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nAnd if another mystery comes to Hollyhock Lane — as mysteries do, like weather — Clara Button will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For courage in quiet lives, always, in Hollyhock Lane and everywhere.\n\nOn Sunday Clara Button walked the hedgerows with no case at all, just the sun and the birds and the good green smell of Hollyhock Lane. A detective, she reflected, is only a neighbor who pays attention.'}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

