cused anyone.\n\nAt dusk she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a coded recipe book in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.\n\nBack in the parlor, Clara Button laid out the evidence: the landlord's tale, the parish gap, the threatening note, the too-perfect description. the vicar Mr. Aldous whistled. 'It's a noose,' they said, 'and the jealous heir tied it.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "The storm that had threatened Willowmere village all week broke at last, rattling the windows of Willowmere village. In the midst of it came a hammering at Clara Button's door: the vicar Mr. Aldous, soaked, with news — the jealous heir had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nClara Button listened to the end, rain running off her cape. Justice, she said quietly, was not the same as punishment; but the truth belonged to everyone in Willowmere village, and it would be told.\n\nThey went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a coded recipe book. The whole sad story lay in the mud."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "On market day Clara Button laid it all before Willowmere village: a coded recipe book, the letters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the jealous heir stood with head bowed and did not deny a word.\n\na coded recipe book was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Clara Button declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nThe constable did his duty, but Clara Button had already done hers: she had made the truth plain, so that no whisper could twist it. the vicar Mr. Aldous squeezed her hand. 'Neatest case I ever saw,' they whispered."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'On Sunday C